    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2056804581515067624",
      "url": "https://x.com/aisarcore/status/2056804581515067624",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/aisarcore/status/2056804581515067624",
      "text": "A man faked his own death in a canoe accident, collected £250,000 in life insurance and hid next door to his own house for years.\n\n– In March 2002 John Darwin paddled his canoe into the North Sea off the English coast and vanished.\n\n– Police launched a massive search. They found fragments of a red kayak. No body. A death certificate was issued.\n\n– Anne collected £250,000 in life insurance and pension payouts and grieved publicly.\n\n– John had not gone far. He was living in the bedsit next door to their family home the entire time, hidden behind a door connecting the two properties.\n\n– Their two sons believed their father was dead for years. Anne looked them in the eye and said nothing.\n\n– Eventually a hidden bedsit was not enough. They wanted a real life.\n\n– They flew to Panama, looked at property and took photos smiling with a local real estate agent.\n\n– In December 2007 John walked into a London police station and claimed he had amnesia. Said he remembered nothing from the last five years.\n\n– Police were taking it seriously.\n\n– Then a woman who had read the news story simply typed \"John Anne Panama\" into Google Images.\n\n– The first result was a photo from a website called https://t.co/HYlRk7UrsN. John and Anne standing together grinning beside a Panamanian property agent. Timestamp: July 4 2006.\n\n– She emailed it anonymously to the Daily Mirror and Cleveland Police the same day.\n\n– The Daily Mirror ran it on the front page under the headline: \"CANOE'S THIS IN PANAMA?\"\n\n– Anne's response when confronted with the photo: \"Yes that's him. My sons will never forgive me.\"\n\n– John got 6 years and 3 months. Anne got 6 years and 6 months.\n\n– John now lives in the Philippines with a wife 33 years younger than him.\n\nThey faked a death, hid next door to their own house, collected £250,000, and moved to Panama. A random woman with a Google search found them in seconds. Their sons found out their father was alive from a newspaper front page.",
